<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Langah Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1445 CE – 1526 CE (approx.) · capital Multan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A sultanate of the Langah tribe that ruled Multan in the southern Punjab for some eighty years in the 15th and early 16th centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1445, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayyid-dynasty">Sayyid</a> rulers of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> lost control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> chose a local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> family leader as ruler. Shortly afterwards Rai Sahra, chief of the Langah tribe, took power and reigned as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Qutb al-Din, founding a dynasty based at Multan.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husain-shah-langah">Husain Shah Langah</a>, the most important ruler, reigned for about three decades, repelled attacks from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahlul-lodi">Bahlul Lodi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and expanded towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> frontier. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> was a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> shrines, notably those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahauddin-zakariya">Bahauddin Zakariya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-rukn-e-alam">Shah Rukn-e-Alam</a>, and of trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/islamic-emirate-of-afghanistan-2021-present">Afghanistan</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-husain-arghun">Shah Husain Arghun</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/arghun-dynasty">Arghun dynasty</a>, ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, besieged and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> around 1526, ending Langah rule. Multan soon passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> and became the seat of a Mughal province.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/langah-sultanate.jpg" alt="Langah Sultanate" width="320" height="311"><figcaption>Image: Netzach · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indie_1525.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1445 CE – 1526 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Multan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Qutb al-Din Langah, Husain Shah Langah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian, Saraiki</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Langahs of Multan, Sultanate of Multan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1486&amp;focus=langah-sultanate" aria-label="Langah Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6535 1075 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Langah Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6855 1331L7018 1385M6855 1331L6832 1282"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7018 1385h0M6832 1282h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6855 1331h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6855 1331h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Multan. <a href="/atlas?year=1486&amp;focus=langah-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
